                <pb facs="0355" n="348" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg348"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9225: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Tsingtao</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sokobin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tsingtao</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-07T11:00:00+08:00">August 7, 1937—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 7—8:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>American and British Admirals in company with British Consul General and myself
                    interviewed the Mayor of Tsingtao yesterday on the present situation in this
                    city. The Mayor reiterated that if the Japanese landed in Tsingtao he could not
                    give up the city without such resistance as he could make.</p>
                <p>To me personally, notwithstanding Mayor’s reassertion that he would resist a
                    landing of Japanese forces, his attitude appeared to be less obdurate than that
                    reflected in his interview with the British Consul General as reported in my
                    telegram of August 3, 9 a.m.<anchor n="66" xml:id="d373-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sokobin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
